// That does NOT port cleanly to C++ for two reasons:
//   1. Diamond: MRIImagesLibrary already derives from CTImagesLibrary, so
//      deriving HighLevel from *both* gives two CTImagesLibrary subobjects.
//   2. Constructors: C++ always runs base-class constructors, and
//      CTImagesLibrary's ctor does real I/O (chdir + nib.load of the image/seg
//      + reorientation). HighLevel's path_to_folder is a directory *of*
//      case folders, not a single image folder, so that load would fail.
//      Python sidesteps this because HighLevel.__init__ never calls
//      super().__init__(); C++ can't skip it.
//
// HighLevel also never actually uses the inherited behavior: it instantiates a
// fresh CTImagesLibrary per subfolder inside _get_all_images_and_segs_from_path
